Z||000003||afRaceR||01-04-2001||02:09 AM||||but duct tape is cheap........what 89 cents a roll or something..<P>only other thing I can suggest is to find somebody that can work with either fiberglass or aluminum and have them make you a battery box. I don't understand why a piece of plastic could cost SOOOO much. There couldn't be that much R&D in it - if it came with the battery cables I would understand a little more.<P>Just try and find a fabricator to make you a box, I can't see more than $30-40 for soemthing like this.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Gold-86SE||01-04-2001||10:17 AM||||<BR>I have been watching the discussion on battery relocation since I have been on the PFF.  The idea I like, the price of the box is okay.  My question is where does one acquire the cables for such a long distance run?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroJoe||01-04-2001||10:32 AM||||you could probably be able to find long enough power cables, 4 gauge or bigger, from any car audio place.  Competition amps and subs require a lot of power, and they usually go straight from the battery, and then the amps are usually mounted in a trunk or rear of the car, so they must be able to get all that power there somehow.  I think you have to relocate the battery when you do a V8 swap with archie.<P>------------------<BR>FieroJoe<BR>c311t@yahoo.com<BR>AIM: Fiero87311, Snowboarder0311<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/profile_1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000006||TheFiero64||01-04-2001||10:59 AM||||I think archie sells a fiberglass box that fits under the spare tire. I know west coast Fieros sells a steel one but it cost way too much for me.||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||01-04-2001||11:02 AM||||since i modified my headlites, I now have a huge empty space where the motors used to be. Ive thought about moving the battery there and getting some longer cables from Jegs. (btw, that is where battery is located in new Sebring) It would give me a little more weight on front wheels, and a little better heat disipation in engine cmpt.||||reg||1
Z||000008||the need for speed||01-04-2001||11:34 AM||||when i relocated my battery i went and got some welders cable it cost me like 15 bucks.i also got archies tray, it is crap had holes in it and it is all crooked did not even fit flush with the car! make your own, fibber glass is and you can start making other things<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fiberglassmaterials.com/Default.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiberglassmaterials.com/Default.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||MikeSchaefer||01-04-2001||06:42 PM||||Yeah, I can't believe how much money someone can get for a little contraption like that.  Does anyone have the measurements for creating one?  Or has anyone ever had a set made on their own?  I would like to get a few machined and see how it could work out.  <BR>Maybe we could sell them for no profit to PFF members.<BR>later,<BR>Mike S||||reg||6
Z||000010||JohnnyK||01-04-2001||07:20 PM||||Needforspeed: Now you done it! Theres going to be some flamage starting up.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||MrPBody||01-04-2001||08:25 PM||||This is a half-baked idea (one of many in my head):<P>I think it should be possible to use a marine battery box.  (Pep Boys sells a cheap one.)  Set the battery box in a hole cut in the panel beneath the spare tire, and support the box with flat steel strip.<P>There may be lots of reasons why this won't work, but I think the basic idea is sound.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||MikeSchaefer||01-04-2001||08:41 PM||||Whoa, thats an awesome idea MrPBody!  I'm going to look into that,<BR>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000013||MikeSchaefer||01-04-2001||08:48 PM||||Any idea which size I should get from this page: <A HREF="http://www.ahoycaptain.com/shop/batteries.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.ahoycaptain.com/shop/batteries.html</A> <BR>we'll need as tight a fit as we can get<P>||||reg||5
Z||000014||MrPBody||01-05-2001||07:46 PM||||This page has a battery size chart: <A HREF="http://www.rtpnet.org/~teaa/bcigroup.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.rtpnet.org/~teaa/bcigroup.html</A> <P>I think Fiero has a Group 75 battery, which is only a little wider than a Group 24.  The Group 24 box <B>probably</B> would fit.  No guarantee.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||mrfixit58||01-06-2001||09:05 AM||||I've seen the marine box trick. I've also seen Archie's and another were the spare was just removed and replaced with the battery. Personally, it just depends on how "stock" you want the install to look when the hood is opened.<P>I, personally, have been thinking of making my own using fiberglass and then molding and blending it to the trunk liner. The difference between a quality job and a hack-job is the time you take for the details (I'm starting to sound like Archie, now.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ).<P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||4
Z||000016||William Federle||01-06-2001||10:15 AM||||I bought a plastic battery tray from Fleet Farm in Menominee Falls Wisconsin for about $3.00. I got a 3' piece of 16 gage angle iron that had 3/8 holes in it every few inches for about $5.00. I cut the angle iron and fabricated 3 brackets. I took the spare and put it in the trunk and mounted the battery tray to the angle iron and the angle iron to the plastic front compartment. I drilled two holes (1/2 " ) in the front bulkhead near where the vacuum pipe goes through and ran the #2 gage red and black wires through. I ran them through the center console keeping clear of the shifting mechanism and through the metal bulkhead in the back behind the computer. I put some plastic tubing around the wires there to protect them where they went through the bulkhead. They came out behind a heat shield. I put wire loom material around them and ran the black one directly to the block where the wire braid is attached. I ran the red one directly to the starter terminal trying to route both to provide as much clearance as possible from hot things. My daughter had provided me with a kit from Summit Racing to do this, but it included a very large battery box. I just used the red wire from it and the battery hold-down hardware. I got the #2 black wire (25 feet of it) from JC Whitney for $29.00. I borrowed a crimper from work to crimp the connectors to the ends of the wire in the engine compartment - the ends at the battery I used bolt-on side terminal clamps from Autozone. ||||reg||1
Z||000017||MrPBody||01-06-2001||10:58 AM||||There you go!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||88formula||01-07-2001||10:04 AM||||Good thread! <P>Just remember that the cable that goes from the battery to the starter is unprotected unless you install a fuse between the cable and the battery. You wouldn’t want a 25' cable that goes through your center console to short out on something.<P>I had a factory battery cable short out on an engine pulley in a Pontiac Bonneville and it almost caused the car to go up in flames. The whole cable turned into a flame ball and I had a hose pouring on it while it did this. At the moment it happened I was standing right next to my car and the hose was right there on the ground. I paniced when I saw the flames coming out of the hood and grabbed the hose. The cable was only three feet long too. With a 25’ cable you would have no chance if that happened, especially with the cable going through your console. <BR><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 01-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000019||William Federle||01-07-2001||11:48 AM||||You know, I worried about that for a while. However, when I thought about it and looked at the cable after it was installed, I see that I routed it well clear of any moving parts and where it goes through a metal bulkhead, it is protected by an additional 1/8" on a side of plastic tubing. It is routed better in the engine compartment than the original cable was. I guess, one additional element of protection would be to make some sort of insulated standoff near the battery and run the cable to that. Then a very heavy duty fusible link could be installed between that and the battery terminal. <P>I'm not making light of this by any means. I had the original cable burn in half on me in this very car, and I thank God that a fire didn't occur. But, like I said, the cable doesn't get very close to anything that could hurt it.||||reg||1
Z||000020||mshill||01-07-2001||01:25 PM||||J.C.Whitney has battery cable:<P>Ga.   Length   Red          Black<BR>6     25ft.    14TB6130T    14TB6131A  $16.95<BR>4     25ft.    14TB6134N    14TB6135B  $19.95<BR>2     25ft.    14TB6138W    14TB6139U  $29.95<P>They also have cable lugs for each gauge wire, but they come in a box of 25, a few more than anyone person will need.<P><p>[This message has been edited by mshill (edited 01-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000021||DaRkLoRD||01-07-2001||01:57 PM||||If you remove the spare tire, and the mounting brackets etc for it, how much room is there in the front comparment?<BR>Not cutting the frame at all, just removing anything that's not necessary that is used for mounting the spare tire.<P>I want to do a dual battery setup, partly for the stereo and other reasons, but also to get more weight on the front wheels..<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||5
Z||000022||theogre||01-07-2001||03:52 PM||||I just wish all you guys moving the batteries would use some plastic conduit or water pipe to run the cables under the car. That will fully suport the cables and deflect most road trash and brush. Something really large would have to hit them to cause a short that way. I think there's enough room to slide the conduit down the side of the fuel tank. (Plastic conduit is gray and tougher than water pipe.) There's no way I'd run that much wire under a car w/o protection. I'd at the very least put the + lead in conduit. (Keep in mind that the battery can dump incredible curent into a short. Usually way more than it's CCA rating.)<P>There are any number of ways to fab the box. Just make sure anything attached to the plastic spreads load over as much area as posible or the plastic may eventually fail.<P>With advances in todays batteries you could even go to a physically smaller battery and retain the same CCA and most other ratings. You aren't bound to using a stock size in the new location.<P>If the battery is open to the spare well, and most setups are, consider removing a portion of the gasket at the top of the well to let out gas from the battery. Todays batteries give off less gas but they do still outgas.||||reg||1
Z||000023||Philphine||01-07-2001||06:41 PM||||would any of these ideas change with a top mount battery? i hate side mounts.||||reg||1
Z||000024||Philphine||01-07-2001||06:45 PM||||would any of these ideas change with a top mount battery? i hate side mounts.<P>also would you try to have a place in the rear for charging/jumping the battery somewhere in back or more convenient than having to remove the spare (i'd want to keep my spare)? access terminals of some kind?||||reg||1
Z||000025||ray b||01-07-2001||07:25 PM|||| do you need two long cables or 1 long and two short cables to use frame unit body as grd. return. one short block to frame other bat to frame up in front comp.  less is more.   ray||||reg||1
Z||000026||William Federle||01-08-2001||07:10 AM||||theogre - Do you think a hole in the bottom or one of the sides of the plastic front compartment would be sufficient to let out the gas from the battery?<P><BR>I ran the negative cable all the way to the engine block just to try to eliminage starting problems due to corroded body connections and to reduce the number of connections between the battery and the starter.<P><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 01-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000027||Vlad||01-08-2001||08:41 AM||||And why can't just fabricate mounts and put the battery in the rear trank.<P>Why not???||||reg||1
Z||000028||DreXteR||01-08-2001||11:53 AM||||Sounds nice mod.<P>Any pics?<P><BR>DreXteR||||reg||1
Z||000029||Will||01-08-2001||01:14 PM||||There's already a drainage hole in the bottom of the front bucket.<P>Check out <A HREF="http://www.cwestco.com/Upgrades/Upgrades.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.cwestco.com/Upgrades/Upgrades.html</A> <P>That setup keeps the spare tire and allows easy use of jumper cables. There is also a link to a page with measurements. I wouldn't pay that much for it, but it is conceptually a good way of doing things. It should be made out of stainless instead of aluminum, though.||||reg||1
Z||000030||theogre||01-08-2001||08:55 PM||||I don't know how much outgas to expect from todays batteries. They aren't as bad as the old ones but if there is a charging system problem or a cell short inside the battery they can gas out really large amounts. It's a tough call to say what would be adiquate ventilation. I've thougth about moving my battery to get it away from all the heat in back. I haven't decided exactly how I'll do it yet.<P>Phil,,, Most people use the side terminal so they can keep the spare in the OE location. You'll likely have to figure out another storage for it if you use a top terminal. That or consider run flat tires. The biggest problem with side terminals is usually caused by people overtightening the terminals. If you use good cable ends and coate them to prevent corosion then you likely won't have problems.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 01-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000031||Black88GT||01-08-2001||09:31 PM||||MR2's have the battery up front.  Maybe we can get some insight from them.  Where did our MR2 friends go anyway?<P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||1
